        "The rivers that flow into natural lakes located in Germany include the following: the Rhine River flows into Lake Constance (Bodensee), which is a natural lake situated at the border of Germany, Switzerland, and Austria; the Danube River flows into several natural lakes along its course, including Lake Constance (Bodensee) and Lake Balaton in Hungary; the Isar River flows into Lake Starnberg (Starnberger See), a natural lake in Bavaria; the Lech River flows into Lake Forggensee, a natural lake in Bavaria; the Eibsee is fed by the Eib River, which is a natural lake in Bavaria; the Ammer River flows into Lake Ammersee, a natural lake in Bavaria; the Walchensee is fed by the Walchen River, a natural lake in Bavaria; the Chiemsee is fed by the Alz River, a natural lake in Bavaria; the Tegernsee is fed by the Mangfall River, a natural lake in Bavaria; the Königssee is fed by the Obersee and the Königsseer Ache, natural lakes in Bavaria; the Lake Constance (Bodensee) is fed by the Rhine River and other smaller streams; the Lake Müritz in Mecklenburg-Vorpommern is fed by the Elde River and other smaller streams; the Lake Schwerin in Mecklenburg-Vorpommern is fed by the River Elde and other streams; the Lake Steinhuder Meer in Lower Saxony is fed by the Steinhuder Mühle River and other streams; the Lake Plön in Schleswig-Holstein is fed by the Schwentine River; the Lake Scharmützelsee in Brandenburg is fed by the River Oder-Spree; the Lake Kummerow in Mecklenburg-Vorpommern is fed by the River Kummerower Mühle; the Lake Großer Plöner See in Schleswig-Holstein is fed by the Schwentine River; the Lake Edersee in Hesse is fed by the Eder River; the Lake Walchensee in Bavaria is fed by the Walchen River; the Lake Chiemsee in Bavaria is fed by the Alz River; the Lake Ammersee in Bavaria is fed by the Ammer River; the Lake Starnberg in Bavaria is fed by the Ammer River and other streams; the Lake Königssee in Bavaria is fed by the Obersee and the Königsseer Ache; the Lake Tegernsee in Bavaria is fed by the Mangfall River; the Lake Schliersee in Bavaria is fed by the Isar River; the Lake Spitzingsee in Bavaria is fed by the Rote Vale River; the Lake Walchensee in Bavaria is fed by the Walchen River; the Lake Eibsee in Bavaria is fed by the Eib River; the Lake Forggensee in Bavaria is fed by the Lech River; the Lake Fuschlsee in Austria is not in Germany; the Lake Wolfgangsee in Austria is not in Germany; the Lake Attersee in Austria is not in Germany; the Lake Traunsee in Austria is not in Germany; the Lake Mondsee in Austria is not in Germany; the Lake Hallstätter See in Austria is not in Germany; the Lake Wörthersee in Austria is not in Germany; the Lake Neusiedler See in Austria and Hungary is not in Germany; the Lake Constance (Bodensee) is the largest natural lake fed by the Rhine River and other streams in Germany.\n\nTherefore, the main rivers flowing into natural lakes located in Germany are: Rhine River",
